; P11 M4 -- what an Asteroids frame costs with the saucer and the sound in it.
;
;   erall                        <-- REQUIRED, see below
;   load "asteroids   (do NOT run the game)
;   load "p11m4
;   p11m4
;
; M4 adds two kinds of cost, and the point of this run is that they are not the
; same kind:
;
;   * A FLAT cost on every frame -- `step.saucer`'s countdown, `heartbeat`'s
;     countdown, `step.sau.shot`'s first comparison.  Three procedure calls and
;     about six statements, whether or not anything is happening.
;   * A cost only on the frames a saucer is ON SCREEN -- its motion, its jink
;     and fire countdowns, three shot pairs, one ship pair, the warble, and an
;     eight-segment outline at 20 statements.  A saucer is up for perhaps a
;     sixth of a level, so this is the peak and not the mean.
;
; So there is a FOURTH measurement point: twelve rocks with a saucer held on
; screen for every frame of it.  M3 was accepted at 69.2 ms at twelve rocks
; against a 71.4 ms budget -- 2.2 ms of headroom -- so the question this run
; answers is whether the peak frame fits in it, and if it does not, which of the
; three levers the design has priced (MAX.ROCKS 12 -> 11 at ~3.0 ms, the shot cap
; at ~2.0, the large rock at 6 -> 5 segments at ~1.4) has to be spent.
;
; THE FIRST RUN OF THIS SCRIPT ANSWERED "NO", AND THE REASON WAS NOT THE SAUCER.
; 73.07 ms quiet and 77.94 with a saucer, `frame = 36.83 + 3.020 n` -- and the
; SLOPE had moved, from M3's 2.860, on a rock pass whose source M4 never touched.
; A global is found by a linear scan of the global table in creation order
; (`find_global`, core/variables.c), and M4 had added 35 constants to the tuning
; block, which sat ahead of the rock state: every list the rock pass reads moved
; 35 slots deeper, eighteen reads a rock, twelve rocks a frame.  The single
; figures in the same run are the proof -- `shot.on` 0.38 -> 0.50 and `thrust`
; 0.99 -> 1.36 with no code change, while `draw.boom`, which is one `arc`
; primitive and almost no interpretation, did not move at all.
;
; That was fixed twice.  First by hoisting the state block above the tuning
; block, which the SECOND run measured at 69.35 quiet and 73.24 with a saucer --
; `frame = 35.85 + 2.792 n`, 3.72 ms off, and M3's frame was 69.21, so the whole
; saucer and the whole PSG arrangement had cost 0.14 ms once the lookup depth was
; paid back.  Then properly, in the interpreter: `find_global` keeps a hash index
; now, the position effect measures zero, and the game-side hoist was reverted.
;
; THIS RUN carries that plus five gameplay changes, two of which are in the hot
; loop: the saucer's shot breaks rocks and the saucer dies against them, which
; are a fourth and fifth test inside `shot.on`.  The host puts them at about 2 %
; of the frame body.  The question is the same one as always -- whether a
; twelve-rock frame with a saucer up still fits in 71.4 ms.
;
; A SAUCER MUST BE KEPT OFF THE FIRST THREE POINTS ON PURPOSE.  `setup.level`
; leaves `sau.wait` at the game-long gap, which starts at 146 frames, and these
; points are 300 frames long -- so a saucer would appear a third of the way through every one of
; them and quietly average itself into a figure that is supposed to be without
; one.  `measure` parks the wait out of reach; `test_the_m4_harness_keeps_the_saucer
; _off_the_plain_points` is what stops that being un-done.
;
; The history this replaces, and it is worth keeping because every number in it
; was a surprise: M1 measured a rocks-only frame at 60.5 ms and fitted
; `30.5 + 2.507 n`.  M2's first run measured 86.4 ms at twelve rocks, nineteen
; over, with the whole miss in the collision pass and the cause in `item` -- a
; walk into a twelve-element list costs ~115 us, and a three-pass frame did about
; 290 of them.  Fusing the passes into one took it to 68.3, the rate went 15 -> 14
; fps, and inlining `wrapc` took another 2.9.  M3's ship test cost 0.245 ms a rock
; and the frame came to 69.2.
;
; THE WORST CASE, NOT A TYPICAL ONE.  Three shots are live on every frame of
; every point -- the game's ceiling, and the case the budget has to be cut
; against.  Since M2's fusion an idle shot is parked off the field rather than
; guarded, so a quiet frame costs the same three comparisons a rock; what the
; worst case still buys is that no shot is ever short-circuited by a hit.
;
; HOW THE BOARD IS HELD STILL.  Every rock's `rrad` is set to 0 for the run, so
; no shot ever hits anything: the rock count stays exactly where `setup.level`
; put it for all 300 frames, no rock splits, and no shot is consumed.  `ship.rad`
; goes to 0 with it, so the ship cannot be hit either -- a death would take the
; ship out of the drawing and put an explosion ring in, and time a frame nobody
; is measuring.  The saucer point zeroes `sau.w`, `sau.h` and `sau.shot.rad` for
; exactly the same reason and by exactly the same means: nothing in the game is
; redefined, it is three lists and four constants set once.
;
; The ship is LANDED for the same reason.  A new ship waits for its spawn point
; to clear before it appears (M4), and a waiting ship is not drawn -- so a run
; that started with one would time a frame with no ship in it until the rocks
; happened to drift clear.
;
; The price of that is stated rather than hidden: a zero box rejects every shot
; on `shot.on`'s FIRST comparison, where a real box (26 steps for a large rock)
; passes the x test for about one pair in six and runs a second comparison.  So
; the rock-pass column below is a LOWER BOUND, low by roughly
;
;   36 pairs * 1/6 * one comparison  ~=  0.3 ms
;
; and a frame in which a shot actually connects is CHEAPER than either, because
; the rock it kills is not drawn.  A lower bound is the right side to err on for
; a number that is about to decide which levers get spent.
;
; Manual refresh, not `sync`: the number wanted is the work, not the cadence.
; `sync` would wait out the rest of the frame and report the wait.  That also
; means the figures here are what the frame COSTS, and the game will run at
; `fps` or slower, never faster.
;
; ONE THING THIS OVER-MEASURES, and it is worth knowing which way: the board it
; builds is all LARGE rocks, and that board cannot occur in play.  A level
; spawns `level.rocks` larges and twelve rocks is only reachable by splitting
; down, so a real twelve-rock board is smaller rocks with fewer segments -- 2.9
; ms cheaper at three starting larges, 1.9 once levels advance to five.  The
; pessimism is deliberate: it is reproducible, it compares with M0 through M3,
; and it errs on the side that does not flatter the budget.
;
; Run it in graphics mode -- `setup.level` leaves the screen there.  In text
; mode `screen_gfx_blit_dirty` returns immediately, so the present column
; reads zero and the frame figure is a Logo body with no present in it, which
; is the correction P9 had to make to its whole first series of numbers.
;
; `erall` is not optional.  MAX_PROCEDURES is a hard 128; the game defines 65
; and this file adds 15, but a workspace still holding another program can run
; out of procedure slots and `load` stops with `out of space` -- the procedure
; table talking, not memory.
;
; The report goes to `p11m4.txt` as well as the screen, because numbers on the
; PicoCalc's display cannot be copied anywhere.  Runs append.

make "p11m4.frames 300

; Four points: 6, 9 and 12 rocks with no saucer, then 12 rocks with one held on
; screen for every frame.
make "p11m4.body (list 0 0 0 0)    ; ms, mean
make "p11m4.pres (list 0 0 0 0)    ; ms, mean
make "p11m4.rock (list 0 0 0 0)    ; ms, mean -- the rock pass alone
make "p11m4.lo (list 0 0 0 0)      ; ms, whole frame
make "p11m4.hi (list 0 0 0 0)
make "p11m4.rocks (list 0 0 0 0)   ; rocks actually on the board, not asked for

; `play.frame` without its `sync`, with a timer around the rock pass.
; THIS MUST STAY IN STEP WITH THE GAME: it is the same statements in the same
; order, and it is spelled out here rather than calling `play.frame` because
; the present has to be timed on its own and `sync` is the last thing
; `play.frame` does.  `test_asteroids.c` pins that this draws what a real frame
; draws and steps what a real frame steps -- including, since M4, that it steps
; the saucer and draws it on exactly the frames the game draws it.
;
; The two `ticks` and the two statements around them are in the body figure,
; and cost about 0.15 ms of it.  That is the price of knowing which half is
; over, and it is smaller than the decision it informs.
to frame.body
  local "t
  poll.input
  make "frame.count :frame.count + 1
  ifelse 0 < :dying [step.death] [ifelse :waiting [step.wait] [step.ship]]
  step.shots
  step.saucer
  heartbeat
  clean
  make "t ticks
  step.draw.all
  make "rockpass ticks - :t
  draw.ship
  if 0 < :sau.on [draw.saucer]
  draw.hud
  reclaim
end

; Three shots, fired 120 degrees apart so they are not one dot travelling
; together, through the game's own `fire` so the turtles are set up exactly as
; play sets them up.
to launch.shots
  repeat :max.shots [
    make "sh (repcount * 120) - 120
    fire
  ]
  make "sh 0
end

; A shot expires after `shot.life` frames.  Topping the life up before each
; frame keeps all three live for the whole run without touching the game's
; countdown: `step.shot` still decrements, it just never reaches zero.  Outside
; the timed body, so it is not in any figure below.
to arm.shots
  repeat :max.shots [.setitem repcount :slife :shot.life]
end

; The saucer point needs a saucer on screen for all 300 frames, and a real one
; crosses the field in 178 -- so it is put back at the middle of its crossing
; before each frame, the same way `arm.shots` tops the shots up.  Its own
; countdowns are left alone: it still jinks, still fires, and its shot is still
; flown by the engine and stepped by the frame, because those are part of what a
; saucer costs.  Outside the timed body.
to hold.saucer
  make "sau.x 0
end

; And it is spawned rather than assembled, so the size, the box and the
; countdowns are exactly what play would have set -- then the boxes are zeroed,
; which is the same trick the rocks get: every pair rejects on its first
; comparison, so the figure is a lower bound and never a death.
to arm.saucer
  spawn.saucer
  make "sau.w 0
  make "sau.h 0
  make "sau.shot.rad 0
end

; `init.game` FIRST, then the rock count: init.game resets `level.rocks` to
; `start.rocks`, so setting it before the call is silently thrown away and
; every point measures a three-rock board.  That is exactly what M1's first
; hardware run did -- all three points came back within 0.03 ms of each other,
; and three identical numbers is what gave it away.  `p11m4.rocks` records what
; was actually on the board so the report can never hide it again.
;
; `:saucer` is true for the fourth point only.  On the other three the appearance
; countdown is parked out of reach, because `sau.first` is 140 frames and a point
; is 300 -- a saucer would otherwise walk into two thirds of a measurement that
; is supposed to be without one.
to measure :k :n :saucer
  local "b local "p local "c local "one local "t0 local "lo local "hi
  init.game
  make "level.rocks :n
  setup.level
  .setitem :k :p11m4.rocks :rocks.alive
  repeat :max.rocks [.setitem repcount :rrad 0]
  make "ship.rad 0
  make "waiting false
  make "sau.wait 999999
  if :saucer [arm.saucer]
  launch.shots
  setrefresh "manual
  make "b 0
  make "p 0
  make "c 0
  make "lo 99999
  make "hi 0
  repeat :p11m4.frames [
    arm.shots
    if :saucer [hold.saucer]
    make "t0 ticks
    frame.body
    make "one ticks - :t0
    make "b :b + :one
    make "c :c + :rockpass
    make "t0 ticks
    refresh
    make "one :one + (ticks - :t0)
    make "p :p + (ticks - :t0)
    if :one < :lo [make "lo :one]
    if :one > :hi [make "hi :one]
  ]
  .setitem :k :p11m4.body :b / :p11m4.frames
  .setitem :k :p11m4.pres :p / :p11m4.frames
  .setitem :k :p11m4.rock :c / :p11m4.frames
  .setitem :k :p11m4.lo :lo
  .setitem :k :p11m4.hi :hi
end

; Printed twice -- once to the file, once to the screen -- so it is one list of
; lines rather than two that can drift apart.  Punctuation is spelled out in
; words: every mark in a printed list is tokenised as a word of its own, so
; `M1+M2` comes out as `M1 + M2` and `--` as `- -`.
; The count printed is the one the board actually carried, read back from the
; game after `setup.level`, not the one this script asked for.
to line.for :k :what
  pr (se item :k :p11m4.rocks [rocks and 3 shots] :what)
  pr (se [  body] item :k :p11m4.body [ms   present] item :k :p11m4.pres [ms])
  pr (se [  of that, the rock pass] item :k :p11m4.rock [ms at least])
  pr (se [  frame] (item :k :p11m4.body) + (item :k :p11m4.pres)
      [ms   min] item :k :p11m4.lo [ms   max] item :k :p11m4.hi [ms])
end

; What one `recycle` costs.  The game recycles when the word table falls below
; `atom.floor` (B25), so how often that happens depends on the board -- which
; is the point of the change, and the reason the word-space figures below are
; the ones to read beside this: together they say how many frames a recycle
; buys HERE.  It has grown at every
; milestone -- 1.3 ms at M1, 2.2 at M2, 4.1 at M3 -- and NOT because the frame
; allocates more: a recycle walks the whole node pool, so it scales with the
; workspace, and M4 is 14 more procedures than M3.  It is the first number this
; run should be read for after the frame itself, because at M3 it was the whole
; of the worst frame's overshoot.
to time.recycle
  local "t0 local "total
  make "total 0
  repeat 10 [
    frame.body
    make "t0 ticks
    recycle
    make "total :total + (ticks - :t0)
  ]
  output :total / 10
end

; Thrust is the only part of a frame that is not in the figures above, because
; it runs only on frames where thrust was the key read and the harness presses
; nothing.  Measured on its own so the worst case -- a player holding thrust at
; full speed, where the clamp branch runs every time -- can be added by hand.
; Since M4 it also gates a note, which is a primitive call inside it.
to time.thrust
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 100 [thrust]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 100
end

; What one `shot.on` costs -- the whole shot-cap lever, priced on its own,
; because since the fusion it is three statements inside the rock loop and no
; timer can get between them.  Cap the shots at two and a third of this comes
; off every live rock, every frame.  Measured against the same parked-or-flying
; shots the run itself used, so it is the common path and not a contrived one.
to time.shot.on
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 100 [ignore shot.on 40 40 26]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 100
end

; What the explosion ring costs, and it is the one M3 cost that is NOT in the
; figures above: it replaces the ship on death frames only.  Timed at `dying` 1,
; which is the ring's last and largest frame, so this is the worst one rather
; than the mean of ten.  `arc` sweeps 4 degrees a segment, so a full circle is 90
; strokes inside one primitive call with no interpreter between them -- the
; question is whether that is a drawing statement's cost or ninety of them.
to time.boom
  local "t0
  make "dying 1
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 20 [draw.boom]
  make "dying 0
  output (ticks - :t0) / 20
end

; The saucer's outline on its own -- eight segments and 20 statements, the
; longest walk in the game, against a large rock's six and 15.  The saucer point
; above carries this plus the saucer's stepping; this says how much of it is the
; drawing, which is the half that no lever except the outline itself reaches.
to time.saucer.draw
  local "t0 local "was
  make "was :sau.on
  make "sau.on 2
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 20 [draw.saucer]
  make "sau.on :was
  output (ticks - :t0) / 20
end

; And the sound, which is new in M4 and is the one cost this design has never
; measured in any form: `sound` is a primitive that touches the PSG, and a frame
; gates one note about every eight frames on the heartbeat alone.  If this is
; expensive the arrangement in section 11 is what changes, so it is worth a
; figure rather than an assumption.
to time.sound
  local "t0
  make "t0 ticks
  repeat 20 [(sound [0 4] :beat.hz.a 90 9)]
  output (ticks - :t0) / 20
end

to p11m4.report
  pr [P11 M4 what an Asteroids frame costs with the saucer and the sound in it]
  pr (se [frames per point] :p11m4.frames)
  pr []
  line.for 1 [no saucer]
  line.for 2 [no saucer]
  line.for 3 [no saucer]
  line.for 4 [WITH a saucer on every frame]
  pr []
  pr (se [the saucer costs]
      ((item 4 :p11m4.body) + (item 4 :p11m4.pres))
      - ((item 3 :p11m4.body) + (item 3 :p11m4.pres)) [ms a frame while it is up])
  pr []
  pr (se [budget at] :fps [fps is] 1000 / :fps [ms])
  pr [M1 rocks only measured 30.5 plus 2.507 n]
  pr [M2 with the ship and shots measured 33.9 plus 2.627 n]
  pr [M3 with the ship test measured 34.86 plus 2.860 n]
  pr [M3 was accepted at 69.2 ms at 12 rocks, worst frame 74]
  pr [M4 first run 36.83 plus 3.020 n, 73.1 quiet and 77.9 with a saucer]
  pr [M4 second run 35.85 plus 2.792 n, 69.35 quiet and 73.24 with a saucer]
  pr [M4 third run 35.36 plus 3.035 n, 71.78 quiet and 75.58 with a saucer]
  pr [that run added the hash index and the arcade saucer rules]
  pr []
  pr (se [one shot.on] :son [ms, times one rock, times every frame])
  pr (se [one thrust] :thr [ms, on frames where it is held])
  pr (se [one explosion ring] :bm [ms, on death frames instead of the ship])
  pr (se [one saucer outline] :sau [ms, on frames a saucer is up])
  pr (se [one sound] :snd [ms, about one frame in eight])
  pr (se [one recycle] :rec [ms, and the game does one when the word table falls below] :atom.floor)
  pr []
  pr (se [nodes at start] :n0)
  pr (se [nodes at end  ] :n1)
  pr (se [after recycle ] :n2)
  pr []
  ; WORD SPACE IS THE ONE THAT RUNS OUT, and until `atoms` existed no run
  ; could report it.  The game dies on the atom table, not on nodes: measured
  ; on the host at the moment the frame loop stops, there are 21,000 free
  ; nodes and 20 free bytes here.  So this is the figure that says whether
  ; `atom.floor` is right on THIS board, and the one to read if the game
  ; ever reports `Out of space` again.
  pr (se [word bytes free] :a0 [at start,] :a1 [at end,] :a2 [after recycle])
  make "spend (:a0 - :a1) / :p11m4.frames
  ifelse :spend > 0 [
    pr (se [a frame spends] :spend [bytes of it, and the floor of] :atom.floor
        [covers] :atom.floor / :spend [frames])
  ] [
    pr [a frame spent no word space -- too few frames to size the interval]
  ]
  pr []
end

to p11m4
  local "n0 local "n1 local "n2 local "a0 local "a1 local "a2
  local "spend
  local "rec local "thr local "son local "bm
  local "sau local "snd
  recycle
  make "n0 nodes  make "a0 atoms
  measure 1 6 false
  measure 2 9 false
  measure 3 12 false
  measure 4 12 true
  make "rec time.recycle
  make "thr time.thrust
  make "son time.shot.on
  make "bm time.boom
  make "sau time.saucer.draw
  make "snd time.sound
  make "n1 nodes  make "a1 atoms
  recycle
  ; Read the recovered figure HERE, not inside the report: printing builds a
  ; `sentence` per line, so a `nodes` evaluated down there reads the report's
  ; own garbage back as if the recycle had under-recovered.  P10's script lost
  ; its first hardware run to exactly that.
  make "n2 nodes  make "a2 atoms
  setrefresh "auto
  textscreen
  stopsound
  open "p11m4.txt
  setwrite "p11m4.txt
  p11m4.report
  setwrite []
  close "p11m4.txt
  p11m4.report
  pr [Also written to p11m4.txt]
end
